          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Form the best five-card poker hand using your two hole cards and exactly one 'path' of three community cards from the cross.",
          gameplay: '1. Betting begins after the hole cards are dealt. 2. The outer cards of the cross are revealed one by one, with a betting round after each. 3. The middle card (shared by both paths) is revealed last for the final betting round. 4. Hand Construction: You can use the vertical column (3 cards) + your 2 hole cards OR the horizontal row (3 cards) + your 2 hole cards. You cannot mix cards from both lines.',
          keyRules: [
            'Must use exactly one path (vertical OR horizontal) plus your 2 hole cards',
            'Cannot mix cards from both lines',
            'The center card is shared by both paths',
            'Standard poker hand rankings apply',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "In the early betting rounds, look for 'connected' hole cards. If you have a Jack and 10, any path with a King, Queen, or 9 becomes highly valuable.",
          intermediate: "Center Card Weight: The center card is the most powerful because it's the only one that completes both possible paths. If you have 'outside' matches early, you are heavily dependent on that center card.",
          advanced: "Pot Odds: Since the community cards are revealed slowly, calculate your 'outs' for each of the two potential paths. A hand that is weak on the horizontal row might be a monster on the vertical.",
          tips: [
            "Fold early if your hole cards don't match any of the first three revealed community cards.",
            'Be wary of high-card strength on the table; if a path has an Ace and King, a pair of Jacks might not be enough.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
